Hackploy challenge package template

Rules for packages under challenges/ for the Hackploy Hub / platform.

Folder structure
---------------
Each package is one folder with a Hub archive, a Hub description file, and
optional editable build sources:

  challenges/
    <package-name>/
      <package-name>.tar.gz
      <package-name>.md
      build/                 (optional author sources)

Example:

  challenges/
    linux-basics-i/
      linux-basics-i.tar.gz
      linux-basics-i.md
      build/

tar.gz contents
---------------
At the root of the archive:

  Dockerfile
  setup.sh
  hackploy.json
  README
  entrypoint.sh            (optional)
  tools/                   (optional compiler sources)
  ...

hackploy.json
-------------
Manifest the platform reads. Prefer a challenges array for multi-station sets.

Required ideas:
  name             lowercase hyphenated set id
  display_name
  description
  credentials.username / credentials.password for SSH labs
  challenges[] with name, display_name, description, difficulty, section,
               flags[{ flag, points, hints? }]

Flags MUST use CN{...}.

Hints are optional per flag. Use a mix in large sets: some with hints, some
without, varied points and easy/medium/hard.

Two ways to earn a flag
-----------------------
1) Checker (practical)
   Hide a keyword. Student runs: ./checker '<keyword>'
   Wrong keyword -> error. Right keyword -> prints CN{...}.

2) Ask (theory)
   Student runs: ./ask
   Interactive questions; on success prints the flag.

Security rules (mandatory)
--------------------------
1) NEVER grant the student account unrestricted sudo.
   Forbidden example:
     user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
   Do not install a sudoers.d drop-in that allows ALL.
   If a station needs a privileged file, create it as root with careful
   ownership/mode during image build. Students solve with user perms.

2) NEVER leave checker or ask as world-readable shell scripts that embed
   plaintext flags or obvious ciphertext next to the answer.
   Preferred pattern:
     - compile small ELF binaries at image build time
     - install them mode 111 (execute-only), owner root
     - students can execute but cannot cat/cp/strings the binary
     - after apt build, purge gcc/headers from the final image

3) Do not install debugging aids students can use to dump binaries
   (gdb, strace) unless the challenge intentionally teaches them.

4) No real secrets, API keys, or private keys in the image.

5) Test: docker build, run container, solve every station, confirm
   sudo -l does not show NOPASSWD ALL, and cat ./checker fails.

Difficulty and coverage
-----------------------
For a full curriculum set, cover many cases in one image:
  - easy / medium / hard
  - low and high points
  - with platform hints and without
  - checker stations and ask stations
  - some on-disk HINT files, some only README

Dockerfile / SSH
----------------
Ubuntu/Debian base, openssh-server, non-root user, expose 22.
Bake lab content at image build (RUN setup.sh) so start is reliable.
Prefer an entrypoint that starts sshd (and any required lab helpers).

Submitting
----------
1. Build and pack under challenges/<name>/
2. Commit and push the Hub repo
3. Platform / Hub sync picks up the package

See also: inside-tar/ for a minimal skeleton, and linux-basics-i/build for a
full multi-station example with compiled checkers.
